<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Listen! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Listen!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Practice tracing and copying letters of the alphabet. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can trace letters of the alphabet and simple familiar words with teacher guidance (e.g., tracing dotted lines to form alphabet letters and familiar words, following teacher guidance). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by matching words to labeled classroom objects (e.g., 'pen' or 'book'). Can copy simple familiar words to label drawings and pictures (e.g., 'sit' or 'pen').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a familiar "School Day" song. While singing, the teacher uses TPR to demonstrate actions and point to classroom objects: "Stand up! (students stand), Listen to the music! (students cup ear), Touch the chair! (students touch a chair), Open the book! (students mime opening a book)." This activates prior knowledge related to the "It's a School Day!" scenario and "Let's Listen!" theme, incorporating vocabulary like 'chair', 'book', 'listen', 'stand up', and imperatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large flashcard with the word "BOOK" written in dotted lines and a picture of a book. The teacher says "It is a book." and slowly traces the letters of "BOOK" with their finger, modeling the correct letter formation. The teacher then models how to hold a pencil correctly and traces the word "book" on a large worksheet, emphasizing the initial sound /b/ in 'book'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by asking simple questions and using visuals.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a book and then a chair) "What is it?" (pointing to the book) - Expected answer: "Book!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a student's pencil grip) "Is this how we trace?" (demonstrating correct vs. incorrect grip) - Expected answer: "Yes!" or "No!"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!":</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful picture chart of a classroom. The chart clearly displays various classroom objects (chair, desk, table, book, window, door) with their corresponding words written underneath. The teacher points to each object and reads the word aloud, e.g., "It is a chair," "It is a desk," ensuring clear pronunciation of /s/ in 'school' and syllable stress in 'teacher' and 'student' as they briefly mention other parts of the school day.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large classroom picture chart with labeled objects (nouns: chair, desk, table, book, window, door; verbs: listen, stand up; adjectives: nice, calm; prepositions: near). This is a visual aid with simple sentences for identification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic Competence - Following instructions: Respond to commands, like 'stand up' and 'listen'. Responding questions: Answering WH-questions like 'What is it?' as the teacher points to objects on the chart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What do you see in the classroom?" (Students point and say 1-2 words, e.g., "chair," "book.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (TPR):</b> "Please, stand up if you see a 'desk'!" (Teacher points to 'desk' on chart). "Please, touch the 'window'!" (Teacher points to 'window' on chart).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Writing.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice tracing and copying familiar words related to the classroom environment.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Imperatives (e.g., 'Trace the word.'), Present simple (e.g., 'It is a book.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> book, chair, desk, teacher, student, listen, touch.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Reinforce initial sounds /b/ 'book', /l/ 'listen', /t/ 'touch' as they trace words.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Tracing Words):</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with large, dotted outlines of target vocabulary words (e.g., "book," "chair," "desk") next to corresponding pictures. The teacher instructs, "Please trace the word 'book'." (Sociolinguistic: Use 'Please'). Students use pencils to trace the words. The teacher circulates, offering individual guidance on pencil grip and letter formation, reinforcing "Nice tracing!" (adjective 'nice').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Copying Words to Match):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of classroom objects (book, desk, chair) and a small word bank at the bottom (book, desk, chair). The task is to copy the correct word from the bank under each picture. The teacher models one example: "What is it? It is a book. Copy 'book' here." The teacher encourages students to say the word as they copy it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check and Labeling):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple drawing of a classroom with three unlabeled objects (e.g., a window, a door, a table). The teacher provides cut-out word labels ("window," "door," "table"). The students work together to match the word labels to the correct objects on the drawing and glue them on. They then point and say, "It is a window," to their partner. The teacher encourages them to use 'Please' when asking for a label.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product - "My School Objects Drawing and Labeling."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Each student receives a blank sheet of paper. The teacher instructs them, "Draw two things you see at school. Then, copy the word for each thing." The teacher provides a visual word bank on the board with words like "book," "desk," "chair," "teacher," "student." Students draw two classroom objects and then carefully copy the corresponding word from the word bank next to their drawing. For instance, a child might draw a book and copy the word "book," and then draw a chair and copy "chair." The teacher emphasizes neatness and correct copying, stating "Make your words nice!" (adjective 'nice'). As students work, the teacher walks around and asks, "What is it?" (WH-question), prompting students to respond orally as they label their drawings. This task allows them to produce written labels based on their drawings, directly connecting to the "It's a School Day!" scenario and requiring them to remember and reproduce the target vocabulary in a written form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in copying familiar words, collaborative interaction (if students help each other locate words in the word bank), and communicative task completion by accurately labeling drawings.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Observation Checklist and Product Evaluation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My School Objects Drawing and Labeling" sheets from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of tracing/copying letters (e.g., letters are clearly formed, follow dotted lines if applicable).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctness of word-to-picture matching (e.g., 'book' labels a book drawing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort in presentation (e.g., drawing is recognizable, words are written neatly).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrated use of target vocabulary (e.g., used 'chair', 'book', 'desk' as labels).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Before leaving, students are asked to show their labeled drawing to a peer or the teacher and respond to one of these prompts:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to a word you traced nicely."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you copy on your paper?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you like tracing words today?" (Students can show thumbs up/down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite word to copy?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well during the writing activities today? Students were very engaged during the tracing and copying activities, especially with the individual drawing and labeling. The use of the word bank on the board was helpful. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? I need to ensure that some students receive more one-on-one assistance with pencil grip and fine motor skills. Perhaps providing larger tracing lines for some words could help. I also noticed some initial confusion with 'teacher' and 'student' words; I will add more visual distinctions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration):</b> In the next lesson, students will consolidate their learning by using the vocabulary (book, chair, desk, listen, stand up) to describe a classroom scene and will integrate their understanding into a larger project involving drawing and verbally explaining parts of their school day. They will mediate information about what they do and see at school, connecting the words they've traced and copied today to real-life objects and actions, fostering their 21st Century Skills project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>